# Stencil Telegram bot — configuration template.
#
# Copy this to `.env` (which is gitignored) and adjust:
#
#   cp bot/.env.example bot/.env
#
# Real environment variables always win over values in `.env`. TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and
# STENCIL_BOT_ALLOWED_USERS are required; everything else has a sensible default.

# REQUIRED. Your bot token from @BotFather (https://t.me/BotFather → /newbot).
# Without it the bot prints a hint and exits. Never commit the real token.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# REQUIRED. WHO may use the bot: a comma-separated list of Telegram user ids. It gates EVERY
# command and upload except /start and /help — an unlisted user gets one plain refusal and
# nothing runs for them. Unset (the default) the bot is off for everyone: anyone who finds it
# can message it, and every command spends the operator's resources — a CLI process, disk in
# the data dir, an outbound fetch to a user-named host, the single LLM API key. Send /start to
# the bot and it reports your id, or ask @userinfobot.
STENCIL_BOT_ALLOWED_USERS=

# Path to the Stencil CLI binary (the pixel engine the bot shells out to, exactly like
# mcp/). Default: auto-discovered — the repo's cli/zig-out/bin/stencil, then `stencil` on
# PATH. Build it with `zig build` in cli/ first.
#STENCIL_CLI=/absolute/path/to/cli/zig-out/bin/stencil

# Optional Redis URL for per-user session state (working image + edits + connections +
# active project), the same store the Go server uses for fan-out. When empty the bot keeps
# sessions in memory (single-instance, lost on restart) — fine for dev and tests.
# Either form is accepted: a redis://[user:password@]host[:port][/db] URL (rediss:// for TLS)
# or StackExchange's own host:port[,option=value] configuration string.
#REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# Scratch directory for per-user working images and rendered results. Default: a
# `stencil-bot` folder under the OS temp dir.
#STENCIL_BOT_DATA_DIR=/tmp/stencil-bot

# Accept self-signed TLS certs when connecting to https collaboration servers (dev only).
# Default false (certificates are verified).
#STENCIL_TLS_INSECURE=false

# Base URL of the SERVED BROWSER APP, e.g. https://stencil.example/app (a path is fine —
# GitHub Pages serves it under one). /link builds its "open in the desktop app" links through
# that app's launch.html bounce page, since Telegram only linkifies http(s) and never a
# stencil:// scheme URL. Default http://localhost:8080 (the dev server, like every other
# surface) — but a bot link is TAPPED ON SOMEONE ELSE'S MACHINE, so point this at a public
# deployment; on the localhost default /link marks its links local-only.
#STENCIL_BOT_BROWSER_URL=https://stencil.example/app

# Max stencil CLI processes run at once, process-wide. Each edit/probe is a separate OS
# process, so this caps CPU/process pressure when many users edit simultaneously; extra work
# waits its turn. Default: the CPU count. Values below 1 are treated as 1.
#STENCIL_BOT_MAX_CONCURRENT_CLI=4

# Max LLM calls in flight at once, process-wide. Each call can run for minutes holding image
# payloads, so this bounds memory and upstream spend; when full the assistant answers "busy"
# immediately instead of queueing. Default 8 (the server's LLM_MAX_IN_FLIGHT default);
# 0 = unlimited.
#STENCIL_BOT_MAX_CONCURRENT_LLM=8

# Per-request timeout (seconds) for collaboration-server REST calls, so a slow/hung server
# can't block an update handler. Default 30.
#STENCIL_BOT_HTTP_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30

# Max size (MB) the bot will download from Telegram for one photo or video, to cap memory/disk
# from an oversized upload. Default 50. A NON-image document (a .json layout or a .stencil
# project) is parsed whole, so it gets a far tighter 4 MB cap — or this value when it is smaller.
#STENCIL_BOT_MAX_DOWNLOAD_MB=50

# Max wall-clock time (seconds) one stencil CLI run may take before it is killed. A scrape
# fetches a page plus N media downloads, so without a bound a hung host could pin a scarce
# STENCIL_BOT_MAX_CONCURRENT_CLI slot indefinitely. Default 120.
#STENCIL_BOT_CLI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120

# How long (minutes) an unreferenced scratch file may sit before the background janitor sweeps
# it. The active image/video are never swept — only orphaned render/layout artifacts. Default 60.
#STENCIL_BOT_WORKSPACE_TTL_MINUTES=60

# ── AI assistant (/prompt) — see llm-contract.md ─────────────────────────────────────
# WHO may use the assistant is STENCIL_BOT_ALLOWED_USERS above — the same global gate every
# other command goes through.

# The same STENCIL_LLM_* keys as pystencil. Which LLM backend /prompt talks to:
#   ollama          — a local Ollama (default), native /api/chat
#   openai-compat   — LM Studio / llama.cpp / vLLM, OpenAI-style /chat/completions
#   stencil-server  — a Stencil collaboration server proxying Anthropic (the API key lives
#                     only in THAT server's environment; the bot never sees it)
#STENCIL_LLM_PROVIDER=ollama

# Endpoint origin for ollama / openai-compat. Defaults follow the provider:
# ollama → http://localhost:11434, openai-compat → http://localhost:1234/v1.
#STENCIL_LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434

# Model name; empty = the provider/server default (e.g. llama3.2-vision for ollama).
#STENCIL_LLM_MODEL=

# Optional API key, sent as `Authorization: Bearer` on openai-compat only (LM Studio needs none).
#STENCIL_LLM_API_KEY=

# stencil-server only: which collaboration server proxies Anthropic. When unset, the invoking
# user's first /connect-ed server is used with their existing session token.
#STENCIL_LLM_SERVER_URL=

# stencil-server with the URL above pinned: the bearer token the bot presents for users who have
# never /connect-ed to that server themselves (a user's own token still wins). Leave empty to
# require /connect. Mint one on the server with POST /auth/token.
#STENCIL_LLM_SERVER_TOKEN=

# ── Selectable chat APIs (/chatapi) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# STENCIL_LLM_PROFILES names the APIs the picker offers; each needs one
# STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_<NAME>_* group. Anything a group omits falls back to the plain
# STENCIL_LLM_* value above, so a profile that only swaps the model is three lines.
# Unset (the default) = no picker: every turn uses the STENCIL_LLM_* config.
#
# The picker is a LIST, never free text: a user selects one of these, and the endpoint
# always comes from here rather than from a chat message.
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILES=claude,llama,lmstudio
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_CLAUDE_LABEL=Claude (via server)
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_CLAUDE_PROVIDER=stencil-server
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_CLAUDE_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:8090
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_CLAUDE_SERVER_TOKEN=
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_LLAMA_LABEL=Llama (local)
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_LLAMA_PROVIDER=ollama
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_LLAMA_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_LLAMA_MODEL=llama3.2-vision
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_LMSTUDIO_LABEL=LM Studio
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_LMSTUDIO_PROVIDER=openai-compat
#STENCIL_LLM_PROFILE_LMSTUDIO_BASE_URL=http://localhost:1234/v1
